✅ Pros of Native Queries

Full SQL Power

Can use advanced SQL features not available in JPQL (e.g., window functions, CTEs, JSON operators, DB-specific functions).

Performance Tuning

Directly leverage database-specific optimizations for faster queries.

Legacy Support

Useful when working with existing SQL queries or stored procedures in a legacy database.

Flexibility

Easier to write for developers who are comfortable with raw SQL instead of JPQL/HQL.

Direct DB Features

Can use vendor-specific extensions (LIMIT, OFFSET, ROWNUM, partitioning, full-text search, etc.).

❌ Cons of Native Queries

Database Vendor Lock-In

Queries are tied to a specific database dialect.

Switching DB means rewriting queries.

Bypass JPA Benefits

No automatic entity mapping, no caching, no dirty checking.

You lose type-safety and object-relational abstraction.

Harder to Maintain

Column/table renames in entities won’t automatically update queries.

Large SQL inside repositories reduces readability.

No Compile-Time Validation

JPQL is validated at startup; native queries only fail at runtime.

SQL Injection Risk

If string concatenation is used instead of parameters.

JPQL handles parameter binding safely.

Manual Mapping Required

For complex joins, you must use @SqlResultSetMapping or DTOs.

With JPQL, entities are automatically mapped.

May Break ORM Optimizations

Hibernate caching, batching, and lazy-loading may not apply to native queries.

⚖️ Conclusion

Use JPQL/Criteria API for most CRUD and entity-based queries.

Use native queries only when:

You need DB-specific features.

You need to optimize performance.

You’re working with a legacy system.